The quantum oscillator has a Hamiltonian of the classical oscillator except just replace the classical position and momentum variables with their quantum operator analogues $p\mapsto \hat p$ and $x\mapsto \hat x$.

The classical Hamiltonian (or total energy) is learnt at school to be kinetic + potential,
\begin{equation}
H=\frac{p^2}{2\mu}+\frac 12 kx^2
\end{equation} 
For the reduced two body system. It is a postulate of quantum mechanics that in the position representation (just a way of formulating things) that wherever you see a $p$ and an $x$ you replace them by $\hat p$ and $\hat x$ where,
\begin{equation}
\hat p =-i\hbar \frac{\partial }{\partial x}, \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ and\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \hat x =\cdot x
\end{equation}
Therefore with these changes in the above classical expression we generate the quantum Hamiltonian of the quantum harmonic oscillator, 
\begin{equation}
\hat H=\frac{\hbar^2}{2\mu}\frac{\partial ^2}{\partial x^2}+\frac 12 kx^2 
\end{equation}
Where $i=\sqrt -1$ so $i^2=-1$ and $--1=1$.

